===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Not [[able]] to be [[placate]]d or [[appease]]d.
#: {{synonyms|en|impacable|q1=obsolete|irreconcilable|unassuageable|unplacable|q4=obsolete|unpleasable}}
#: {{antonyms|en|appeasable|assuageable|pacable|pacifiable|placable}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=III|scene=iv|page=269|passage=He is knight dubb'd with vnhatche'd Rapier, and on carpet conſideration, but he is a diuell in priuate [[brawl|brall]], soules and bodies hath he diuorc'd three, and his incenſement at this moment is ſo '''implacable''', that ſatisfaction can be none, but by pangs of death and ſepulcher: Hob, nob, is his word: giu't or take't.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J[ohn] Stagg|authorlink=John Stagg (poet)|chapter=Arthur’s Cave. A Legendary Tale.|title=The Minstrel of the North: Or, Cumbrian Legends.{{nb...|Being a Poetic Miscellany of Legendary, Gothic, and Romantic, Tales.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed by Hamblin and Seyfang,{{nb...|Queen-Street, Cheapside}}, for the author, and sold by J. Blacklock,{{nb...|Royal Exchange.}}|year=1810|page=105|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fHITlcm0MJwC&pg=PA105|oclc=7000697|passage=[I]n the reign of [[w:Henry II of England|Henry the Second]], a body happening, by chance, to be dug up near Glastonbury Abbey, without any symptoms of putrefaction or decay, the Welch, the descendants of the Ancient Britons, tenacious of the dignity and reputation of that illustrious hero &#91;{{w|King Arthur}}&#93;, vainly supposed it could be no other than the body of their justly-boasted Pen-Dragon; and that he had been immured in that sepulchre by the spells of some powerful and '''implacable''' inchanter.}}
# [[impossible|Impossible]] to [[prevent]] or [[stop#Verb|stop]]; [[inexorable]], [[unrelenting#Adjective|unrelenting]], [[unstoppable]].
#: {{synonyms|en|relentless|unremitting|unyielding}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James D. Hornfischer|authorlink=James D. Hornfischer|chapter=The Giants Ride|title=Neptune’s Inferno: The U.S. Navy at Guadalcanal|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Bantam Books}}|year=2011|isbn=978-0-553-80670-0|edition2=trade paperback|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=Bantam Books|year2=2012|page2=345|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=oPE5VMv1NnkC&pg=PR274|isbn2=978-0-553-38512-0|passage=The battleships ''[[w:USS Washington (BB-56)|Washington]]'' and ''[[w:USS South Dakota (BB-57)|South Dakota]]'' pushed through the sea with an '''implacable''' ease.}}
# [[adamant|Adamant]]; [[immovable]].
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|reprint=1825|page=84|passage=Indeed ''Cain'' hated his Brother, becauſe his own works were evil, and his Brothers righteous; and if thy Wife and Children have been offended with thee for this, they thereby ſhew themſelves to be '''implacable''' to good; and thou haſt delivered thy ſoul from their blood.}}

